      "note": "About one minute. The goal is reliable\nmulti-step work, not just plausible text.\n\nThe crafting game gives a clear success\ncheck. The pickaxe is a simplified\nillustration.\n\nRLMs allow helper calls, but the recent\ncrafting tests isolate one model using\ntools. Do not claim a demonstrated benefit\nfrom recursion."
    },
    {
      "idx": 1,
      "label": "2",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "RL is not a total failure. Some runs\nimproved answer delivery or selection; the\neffect often weakened on new examples or\ndid not beat extra example-based training.\n\nIn crafting, a rewarded run can contain\nrejected actions. The final success does\nnot identify the useful steps. This is a\nplausible problem, not a diagnosis proven\nacross all experiments."
    },
    {
      "idx": 2,
      "label": "3",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "The teacher already knows the recipe chain\nand plans before making the demonstration.\nThe discovery teacher asks the game for\nthe recipe and uses its answers to choose\nthe route. Both show lookups.\n\nSuccesses out of32 attempts: first\nrepeat,5 vs15; second repeat,3 vs10. Each\nrepeat tests16 goal settings twice.\n\nSmall, related tasks in one game. Training\nexamples also differ in order and text."
    },
    {
      "idx": 3,
      "label": "4",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "In the code-assisted setup, the model\nstill chooses the item and amount. Code\nreplaces the ingredient list using a\nrecipe already looked up. It does not plan\nor reveal a hidden recipe.\n\nOn the first additional goal panel,\nsuccess rose from7/16 to10/16. All16 pairs\nare known:3 improvements,0 regressions,13\nties. Calls fell571 to460.\n\nThis is one new group in the same game,\nnot another domain. No model training\noccurred for this comparison."
    },
    {
      "idx": 4,
      "label": "5",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Running now: both teaching methods, with\nand without code assistance, on new goal\ngroups and recipe settings. These are\nevaluations of existing models, not new RL\ntraining.\n\nIf the teaching gap shrinks, execution\nreliability may explain part of it. If it\npersists, route choice remains a possible\nexplanation.\n\nProposed next RL experiment: compare\nreward learning with and without\nassistance. The design is not fixed.\nLater, study when the model should\ndelegate or break a task down again."
